11.0.0 — loops in time on their own, drawn longer, and Arrange that follows the song

New since 10086, built by the Echoel Studio run; nothing here is device-verified yet. Each point
answers the recording on 10085.
- An imported audio loop follows the song's tempo at once, its pitch unchanged: the tempo heard
  in it becomes its native tempo, and a tempo in its file name ("jungle 132", "break_174bpm")
  settles short loops and the octave. A file with no steady pulse and no tempo in its name plays
  as imported.
- Slice to Pads places the pattern at the loop's tempo, heard or named: every hit on the song's
  grid even when the file has a tail. The Slice section says which tempo it used.
- Draw a loop longer: drag the right edge of an audio region past the end of its file and it
  repeats up to where you let go, the last pass cut on the magnet's line. One undo step,
  "Loop Region".
- Arrange turns the page with the playhead while the song plays. The new button in the zoom bar
  (Follow Playhead) turns it off.
Limits as in 10086: several notes are chosen with the Select More switch; the note editor zooms in
four sizes; the drum step grid has no zoom strip; a sample click above 300 BPM is cut by the next
one. Session clips in the Session grid still play at the file's own speed.

Previous: 10086. A green commit that changes this file uploads build 10000 + run number. A test
list, not a source for claims (ContentPipeline/CLAIMS.md).

One ask, as for 10083 to 10086. Start a screen recording, open the app, and try to make sixteen
bars in two to five minutes: a beat, a bass line or a chord, then Play. No list and no protocol; stop wherever
it stops being possible or pleasant. The recording is the answer. What gets in the way in it is
the next work, in the order it happened.

The five checks of 10079 stay open and unchanged and rest until that recording exists.
